+# G2 stage scenes: the five benchmark scenes, the battle on the terrain grid, weather and time of day
+
+Stage agent scenes, branch `g2/scenes` (cut from `g2/trunk` 21a181e), 2026-11-11 to 2026-11-12 (an interrupted run
+continued from its own clone and commits). Scope: assemble the five benchmark scenes of D-078 QM8 on the integrated G2
+tree and the remaining DR49 priorities (item 5: battle on the terrain grid, aftermath cinematics, weather), wire them
+into the Victus harness, and capture them. MonoGame 3D only, presentation only. All numbers below were measured in
+this container on **llvmpipe (Mesa 25.2.8 software GL with LLVM 20.1.2 on an Intel Xeon at 2.10 GHz, 4 logical CPUs,
+15 GB RAM; one-minute load average 2.3–4.2 while the costs of §7 ran alone): llvmpipe, not Victus evidence**. No
+hardware GPU ran this code. Quality references were used as references only; nothing player-facing names another game
+(D-069).
+
+**Verdict, honestly.** The five benchmark scenes exist, the harness measures them by default in its slots A–E, and
+the canonical set of 1920×1080 accumulated stills was rendered and looked at (§5). Side by side with G1 (the sheets of
+§5 and §9) the step is large: human bodies with faces, eyes, beards and expressions instead of capsules, period kit with
+painted and worn PBR materials, a forest of real tree models, ground cover, a river, a village and a stone stronghold
+under an analytic sky with cascaded shadows and AO, a 2,100-soldier battle in which every soldier is one entity of the
+prototype resolver's own trace, and an aftermath derived from that battle's recorded outcome, in both states of the
+open death-content setting. My reading of the stills against the mandate's floor (GTA San Andreas-class, a quality
+reference only): in geometry, materials and lighting most frames are past it; in motion, crowd behaviour, faces at the
+closest framings, smoke, the far meadow seen against a low sun (it reads as sand) and the life of the settlements they
+are not yet convincing (§10), and the longer-term
+battlefield ambition (Total War: Troy-class, also a reference only) is far off: thousands of soldiers read as clusters
+of small figures, the crowd does not fight body to body and the land beyond the 1 km map is a flat plain. The Director
+judges; none of this is production quality, and none of it says anything about performance on the Victus.
+
+## 1. What exists now
+
+Five registered scenes in `Gfx.Demo/Scenes/Bench`, all on the env stage's temperate battlefield (the generated grid,
+its forest of ez-tree trees, the river, ground cover, boulders and the Iron Age village; `reports/env.md`), lit by
+render1's sky and PBR and render2's cascades, AO, contact shadows and crowd (`reports/render1.md`, `reports/render2.md`).
+The harness binds them to its slots by default (§6).
+
+| Scene | Slot | What it shows | Driven by |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| `bench-a` | A, soldier close-up | A hoplite (Corinthian helmet with crest, linen corselet, painted aspis, spear) and his bare-headed commander (bronze cuirass, a bronze-headed spear, the stage-kit hero aspis on his arm), three more of the line and a standard, on the meadow by the village, all in the authored hoplite guard. Close-ups of faces, eyes, skin, beard and kit; the commander's expression runs grim → battle cry → calm over the scene's first seconds (MakeHuman face pose units) | scripted presentation (authored kit clips only, no CMU motion) |
+| `bench-b` | B, formation | 320 instanced soldiers of both forces in four blocks of 10 × 8 with a standard and an officer before each block (full kitted characters), facing each other across 36 m of meadow, the village behind one force. The harness's crowd sweep sets the count (more blocks in more lines) | scripted presentation (idles) |
+| `bench-c` | C, large battlefield | the 2,100 soldiers of a battle the prototype TacticalResolver fought on this terrain grid, each soldier one entity of its trace: position, state (fighting, routing, surrendered, fallen, escaped) and blows; a vista of the field at the clash and the clash itself at noon, golden hour and dusk, the advance, the melee and the rout. The clash and melee shots look where the trace's blows fall thickest (`DensestFighting`: the 10 m cell with the most melee Fire events around the shot's time, ties to the lower cell, tested tie-dense) | the battle trace (§2) |
+| `bench-d-shown`, `bench-d-cutaway` | D, cinematic | the aftermath of the same battle as its trace records it (§3): a crane over the field, the prisoners, the surrender (the victors' commander raises his sword over a defeated officer who kneels with his weapons on the ground), the officer's face, the commander against the sky, the standards (the victor's raised, the defeated's on the ground), and either the fallen or a cut-away; smoke from fires and dust | the trace's final state and result |
+| `bench-e` | E, campaign environment | the stronghold on its high ground with the forest and fields beyond at noon, golden hour and dusk; a higher view from behind the village over the fields and forest to the stronghold; the village; the fortification; the river from over the water. A benchmark capture only: the campaign map is unchanged (D-079 Q7) | presentation only |
+
+Weather and time of day (§4) apply to every scene through render switches, so the same scenes and cameras serve every
+look and the harness measures whatever the switches select.
+
+## 2. The battle on the terrain grid (scene C; DR49 item 5)
+
+`tools/traceexport` (outside `M7Gfx2.slnx`, never shipped) references `prototypes/m7-sprint1/Proto.M7` read-only and
+**modifies no simulation code**: it builds a battle setup, runs the resolver's own engine
+(`new TacticalBattle(setup, new KnowledgeAi(), new KnowledgeAi())`, the engine behind `TacticalResolver.Resolve`)
+through its public surface and records what it publishes. `scripts/export-battle-trace.sh` runs it; `--check` re-runs
+it and compares the trace's content hash with the manifest (re-run 2026-11-12: `OK, content hash 7670DF68…331A equals
+the manifest`, 87 s of export on llvmpipe's host CPU).
+
+- **The grid.** The G2 authoritative battlefield grid (the env temperate fixture: 256 × 256 cells of 4 m, BTG1 sha256
+  `be39da87…`) is downscaled by a fixed integer rule to the tactical slice's 20 m cells: 51 × 51 cells, 1,020 m square;
+  5 × 5 fine cells per battle cell; class by majority (deep water across a cell → Water, shallow or bank → Ford; ties to
+  the lower code), height the floor of the mean. The env stage's Iron Age village enters as Building cells. **This
+  mapping is a PROTOTYPE ADAPTER of the tool, not a ruled design.**
+- **The armies.** 21 heavy-infantry formations of 50 per side (the proposed heavy-infantry cap): 2,100 entities. Lines
+  of up to 11 blocks, 15 m frontage with 4.5 m gaps, 30 m between lines, the front lines 170 m apart, on the most
+  open, dry and level 17 × 13-cell site of the grid (cell (8, 41), battle axis north–south, integer score 14,468).
+  Each side's objective is the other's front-line centre. Seed 7.
+- **The result** (the resolver's): SideEliminated, side 1 eliminated, side 0 holds the field, 23 pulses, 1,334 s of
+  battle; 668 snapshots at the 2 s step. At the end: 169 of side 0 still fighting, 559 surrendered (408 of side 1 and
+  151 of side 0, recorded so by the trace), 769 fallen, 603 escaped.
+- **The trace.** Format `m7gfx-battle-trace/1` (G1's) with a header (the battle-grid frame and the result). The JSON
+  is 23,212,719 bytes at this size, so the presentation's reader re-encodes it into a compact binary form (`G2BT`: gzip; delta
+  positions as zigzag varints; every value an exact integer): 681,135 bytes, the same content hash
+  (`7670DF68…331A`). `BattleTrace.Load` reads either form.
+- **The presentation reads the trace only.** `TraceCrowd` (one instanced stage-chars soldier per entity) takes each
+  soldier's position (linear between snapshots), state and blows from the trace and nothing else. What the trace does
+  not decide is cosmetic and labelled so in the code: facing between moves, which idle or guard plays, the kit of each
+  formation and every look (from the entity id). The trace and the grid are never written (tests hash both).
+
+## 3. The aftermath plan (scene D; DR40, DR41)
+
+`AftermathPlan.From` derives the cinematic from the trace's recorded outcome, pure and tested on synthetic traces:
+
+1. The victor is the resolver's `holdsField` side. With none, there is no victory beat and no raised standard (one
+   sombre crane over the field).
+2. The surrender beat exists only if the defeated side has Surrendered soldiers at the end, and its officer **is** one
+   of them (his position and state are the trace's).
+3. The two hero characters stand where two of the trace's soldiers stand: the clearest surrender stage, a victors'
+   standing soldier and a defeated prisoner 1.8–4.5 m apart with the fewest other soldiers on their feet or knees
+   within 4 m (ties: the lower ids; without such a pair, the victors' soldier nearest the prisoners and the prisoner
+   nearest him). Here: entities 611 and 1906, with nobody else within 4 m.
+4. **Death content** (DR41) is a presentation setting whose default is **open** (D-079 Q12): `DeathContent` has no
+   default value (its members start at 1, so `default(DeathContent)` is rejected) and both states are separate
+   registered scenes. `Shown`: the fallen lie where the trace says they fell and the last beat looks at the densest
+   cluster of them. `CutAway`: no dead soldier is drawn anywhere and the last beat cuts away to the smoke and the sky.
+   Nothing else differs: the trace, the outcome and beats 1–6 are identical (a test compares them).
+
+The beats are camera moves (crane, dolly, push-in, close-ups, low angles); each close shot's camera is chosen among
+candidate positions by counting the soldiers across its sight lines (a solver with a fixed candidate order and integer
+costs; ties by order, tested tie-dense). The harness binds slot D to `bench-d-shown`, the state that draws more, as the
+**measurement's upper bound only**; `--bind D=bench-d-cutaway` measures the other state. That binding is not a product
+default.
+
+## 4. Weather and time of day
+
+Off by default: every term 0 renders the frame of the earlier stages (measured 2026-11-12: 12 captures of
+`terrain-temperate`, `chars-forces` and three `env-temperate` shots at 640×360, rendered by the stage's base build 21a181e
+and by this branch's final build, are byte-identical PNGs). Switches (`--set`):
+`weather=clear|overcast|mist|rain` or the terms `cloud`, `wet`, `rain`, `mist` (0..1).
+
+- **Cloud** blends the sky table towards an overcast sky, dims the direct sun and fades the sun disc.
+- **Wet** darkens porous dielectrics (water in the pores) and gives every surface a water film (rougher surfaces gloss
+  up, upward faces more than vertical ones, but a rough surface keeps at least half its roughness: only the puddles
+  become mirrors): terrain, foliage, skin, cloth, kit and structures. (A first version let the meadow go nearly
+  mirror-smooth; under the bright overcast sky the whole field read as snow in the rain captures, so it was changed
+  and the weather looks re-captured.)
+- **Rain** adds puddles on level terrain and camera-surrounding rain streaks (24,000 quads in a wrapped box, lit by the
+  sky; no random state).
+- **Mist** thickens a low height fog and the haze (mist 0 returns the scene's fog bit for bit).
+- **Smoke and dust** (scene D): soft lit billboards whose every puff is a pure function of its index and the scene
+  time, so stills, replays and the benchmark agree. Each puff is shaded as a soft sphere (lighter on its sun side,
+  darker below and away from the sun) with a forward-scattering lobe, and fades out near the camera. Dust is a thin
+  ground layer: its opacity follows its optical depth along the view (1/sin of the view elevation), so it is dense at
+  eye level and faint from the crane above, where round billboards would otherwise read as blots on the field.
+
+Presets: overcast (cloud 0.85), mist (cloud 0.45, wet 0.35, mist 1), rain (cloud 0.93, wet 1, rain 1, mist 0.35).
+Hours (`BenchHours`): noon 12.5 h (sun about 61° up), golden hour 17.9 h (about 9°), dusk 18.62 h (about 1°). Scenes B,
+C and E each have shots at all three hours.
+
