+## 10. What still looks placeholder
+
+Plainly: none of this is production quality. The scenes are a big step from G1's placeholder art (the sheets of §5),
+and what still looks placeholder is:
+
+- **Motion.** The crowd soldiers of B–D move by the stage-chars CMU retargets (PROTOTYPE-ONLY) and play one clip or
+  a blend of two; there is no foot IK on slopes and no collision, so at the contact line spears pass through shields
+  and bodies, and kneeling prisoners keep their spears (the crowd meshes carry their weapons). Formations in B only
+  idle. Scene A's cast and the heroes of B and D hold the authored kit clips (guards, victory, surrender), which are
+  keyed poses with a breathing loop, not captured motion.
+- **Close-ups (A, D).** Faces are the stage-chars MakeHuman identities: skin from a 512-texel albedo with procedural
+  detail, a painted hair cap with shells and cards, simple mouth interiors. At the closest framings the skin is smooth
+  and the hair reads as a cap. Expressions are blends of a few face pose units. The commander's bronze cuirass has a
+  ragged edge at the shoulder straps when the arms are raised (its armholes stretch with the skin weights), and his
+  aspis rides 40 mm off the forearm so his broad shoulder stays behind it (a presentation offset, not a fit).
+- **Crowds (B, C, D).** Beyond a few metres soldiers are the outfits' reduced meshes (render2's levels); at vista
+  distance a formation is a field of small silhouettes. All 2,100 soldiers of C are heavy infantry with spears or
+  swords: the battle has no missile troops or cavalry, and kits are four per force by formation. The era mixes: scene
+  A is a Classical hoplite line, B–D a medieval kit.
+- **The aftermath (D).** Smoke and dust are soft billboards, not volumes; seen against a low sun a plume still reads
+  as a glowing soft column rather than billowing smoke, and against the evening sky of the cut-away the two plumes
+  read as dark red-brown smudges. Dead soldiers lie in ground cover that partly hides them; there is no trampled
+  ground, no blood, no wreckage beyond dropped shields. The victors' soldiers whom the trace records as Surrendered
+  kneel like the defeated (the literal reading of the trace). The hero pair's two-shot is busy with soldiers standing
+  between.
+- **Settlements (E).** The stronghold is the env structure kit's walls, towers and houses (tiling stone, plaster and
+  tile materials), on a bare cleared glacis; the village huts are simple cones; nobody lives in either (no people,
+  animals, hearth smoke). Beyond the 1 km map the land is the render1 far field (a flat plane and a band of hills).
+- **Weather.** Rain is thin camera-facing streaks (no splashes, drips or wet-darkened cloth that changes over time);
+  puddles appear only on level terrain; the overcast sky is a uniform blend, not clouds; mist is a height fog with no
+  light shafts; trees sway the same in every weather.
+- **Into a low sun** (scene C's golden-hour vista and clash, both looking towards the sun: the soldiers' shadows point
+  at the camera) the meadow beyond the near ground cover reads as sand dunes, a flat warm beige with no green; at noon
+  and at dusk, and in the golden-hour shots of B and E, the same terrain reads as grass. Diagnostic renders of the
+  golden-hour vista at 960×540 (`--capture bench-c --shots vista-golden --set haze=1`, `aerial=0`, `groundcover=0`;
+  not committed): the beige stays with the haze at 1 instead of the scene's 3 and with aerial perspective off, and
+  with ground cover off the near meadow turns the same beige. So it is the terrain's own shading under the low sun,
+  beyond the reach of the ground cover that gives the near meadow its green, with the cascade acne's contour lines
+  (next item) adding dune-like ripples. Not fixed here: the terrain material and the ground cover's reach are
+  render1's and env's, and changing them would move their frames.
+- **Shadows** at golden hour show cascade acne: fine contour-like bands on smooth slopes facing the low sun (scene C's
+  rout and vistas, D's crane). Accumulated stills soften them but do not remove them; with the single shadow map they
+  are gone, so they come from the cascades (render1's `CascadedShadowMap`: a constant 1.6-texel depth bias per cascade
+  and a normal offset sized by cascade 0's texel). A per-cascade normal offset or a slope-scaled bias is the fix; it is
+  not made here because it would move every earlier stage's frames (the weather-off identity of §4 would no longer be
+  an identity).
+- **Beyond the map.** The battlefield is the 1 km generated grid; past its edge the land is render1's far field (a flat
+  plane and a band of hills). High and steep views show the flat plane as a featureless, hazy plain, so the vistas of
+  C and E are framed low enough to keep the horizon and sky at the top; scene C's noon vista and the higher views still
+  show some of it.
+
+## 11. Limitations and risks
+
+- **No hardware evidence.** Every number is llvmpipe on a shared machine; only the Victus run measures these scenes on
+  the reference GPU. Loads of 25–60 s in the canonical captures here are mostly the tree impostor bake on the CPU
+  rasterizer (22–43 s of it).
+- **The battle adapter is a prototype choice.** The grid downscale, the village as Building cells, the site search and
+  the deployment are this tool's, not a ruled design; a different seed or site gives a different battle. The resolver
+  ran unmodified, so the battle shows what the prototype resolver does today (for example 151 soldiers of the winning
+  side end the battle Surrendered).
+- **`G2BT` is a prototype format** for this presentation's traces, not a save or replay format.
+- **Scene D's binding.** Slot D measures the Shown state (the upper bound); the product default of the death-content
+  setting is open (D-079 Q12) and nothing here decides it.
+- **CMU motion** is PROTOTYPE-ONLY: every capture of A–D says so; shipping needs authored or licensed motion.
+- **Weather is a look**, never read by any simulation, and it is not tied to any campaign weather state.
+- **Era mix and content gaps** (no ranged or mounted troops in C) limit what the benchmark exercises: archery volleys,
+  horses and siege engines are not measured.
+
+## 12. Sim/presentation boundary
+
+- **No simulation code changed.** `git diff 21a181e -- prototypes/m7-sprint1` is empty; the exporter references
+  `Proto.M7` read-only and drives the resolver only through its public surface. Nothing outside `prototypes/m7-gfx2`
+  changed, and G1 is untouched (`git diff 1601c74 -- prototypes/m7-gfx1` is empty).
+- **Presentation reads, never writes.** The scenes read the authoritative grid and the battle trace; `BattleTrace` has
+  no mutators and a test updates the trace-driven crowd at three snapshot times and checks the trace hashes the same
+  afterwards (§8). Weather, time of day, smoke, the aftermath plan and every camera are presentation only (DR42); the
+  hero characters of scene D take two trace soldiers' positions and states and nothing else (DR39, DR42).
+- **Rendering does not feed back.** No value computed by the renderer reaches the trace, the grid or the exporter; the
+  exporter's only inputs are the fixture grid, the village plan (a presentation plan, entering as Building cells: the
+  PROTOTYPE ADAPTER) and its own constants.
+- **Determinism.** `scripts/check-gfx-determinism.sh` (integer-only scan of `Gfx.Proc/Authoritative`): OK, 4 files.
+  The exporter is integer-only; its output is reproducible (`--check` re-ran it: the same content hash). Smoke puffs
+  and rain streaks are pure functions of their index and the scene time; the aftermath and vantage solvers use integer
+  costs with a fixed candidate order, and `DensestFighting` counts blows per cell in a sorted map (ties to the lower
+  cell; a tie-dense test and three bounded mutants, each killed by that semantic test: the tie-break turned to `>=`,
+  the time window widened a hundredfold, Damage events counted; each mutant's test run bounded by `timeout 600` s, the
+  clean test taking 15 ms in the final Release run; the mutants failed it in 75–116 ms).
+
